+
+## Built-in filter categories
+
+Prompt templates can opt in to groups of generally useful filters with the
+`builtin_filters` argument. It is accepted by `@outlines.prompt`,
+`Prompt.from_str`, and `Prompt.from_file`:
+
+```python
+@outlines.prompt(builtin_filters=["string_utils", "format_utils"])
+def describe(name, data):
+    """{{ name | title }}: {{ data | json }}"""
+```
+
+The available categories are `string_utils` (`upper`, `lower`, `title`,
+`reverse`, `truncate`, `pad_left`, and `pad_right`), `math_utils` (`add`,
+`subtract`, `multiply`, `divide`, `round`, `abs`, `min`, and `max`), and
+`format_utils` (`json`, `csv`, `indent`, `wrap`, `escape`, and `unescape`).
+Selected filters are also available to templates included or inherited by a
+file prompt. Existing Outlines filters remain available.
+
+Passing `None` or an empty list selects no additional category, and duplicate
+categories are harmless. Construction fails with `ValueError` when a category
+name is unknown. `truncate` rejects negative lengths; padding uses Python's
+one-character fill rule. CSV output is one standard quoted row without a line
+terminator, while HTML escaping includes quotation marks.
